                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#example"} Example
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#remark"} Remark
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Skeletal geometric morphisms
            are those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            that preserve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation sheaves
            and therefore play a role in the descriptions of classes of toposes like e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+toposes"} De Morgan toposes
            in whose definitions the negation participates.
          %p
            The notion of a skeletal geometric morphism can be viewed as a weakening of the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+geometric+morphism"} open geometric morphism
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f :  \mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{E}
            is called
            %em skeletal
            if the following equivalent conditions hold:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                restricts to a geometric morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{F}) \to Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
                \.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
                preserves
                = succeed "-dense" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            %li
              %p
                For any subobject
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ↣
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'\rightarrowtail A
                in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℰ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi A
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg f^\ast(A')=\neg f^\ast(\neg A')
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Sub
                          %mi ℱ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub_\mathcal{F}(f^\ast(A))
          %h2#example Example
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Inverse images of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+geometric+morphism"} open geometric morphisms
                are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+functors"} Heyting functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
                and, therefore,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em open geometric morphisms are skeletal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean
                codomain are open (
                %a{:href => "#J02"}> Johnstone 2002, p.612
                ), hence skeletal.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subtopos"} Dense subtoposes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\hookrightarrow \mathcal{E}
                are precisely those subtoposes with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Sh_j(\mathcal{E}))=Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
                (cf.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "dense+subtopos#negdense"} this proposition
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The following two propositions concern skeletal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} inclusions
            (cf. Johnstone (
            %a{:href => "#J02"}> 2002, p.1007
            )):
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              An inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\hookrightarrow \mathcal{E}
              is skeletal iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ext
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext(j)
              , the
              = succeed "-closure" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ↣
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\rightarrowtail 1
              , is a
              = succeed "-closed" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+object"} subterminal object
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \: First, notice that in general for a topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            a subterminal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is
            = succeed "-closed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            = succeed "-sheaf" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is always a
            = succeed "-sheaf." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is
            = succeed "-closed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            = succeed "-sheaf." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
          %p
            Now assume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            skeletal. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext(j)
            is a
            = succeed "-sheaf" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            preserves them, it is also a
            = succeed "-sheaf" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %p
            Conversely, assume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext(j)
            is a
            = succeed "-sheaf" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            = succeed "-sheaf," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\cap Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
            but this coincides with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Sh_j(\mathcal{E}))
            because as a subtopos of the Boolean
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
            the intersection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\cap Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
            is Boolean and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})\cap Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ext
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext(j)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{E})
            and there can be only one such dense Boolean subtopos.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              of skeletal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} inclusions
              is the smallest class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              of geometric morphisms such that:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  contains
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subtopos"} open inclusions
                  and,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  is closed under precomposition with
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subtopos"} dense inclusions
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                  dense,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in\Gamma
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g
                  composable, follows
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi fg
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi Γ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fg\in\Gamma
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ▪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p The following exhibits the link between skeletal morphisms and Booleanness:
          #Boolean_skeletal.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is Boolean iff all geometric morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{E}
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              are skeletal.
          %p
            %strong> Proof
            \: When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is Boolean it coincides with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(\mathcal{E})
            hence
            = succeed "-sheaves" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            trivially have to land there.
          %p
            Conversely, assume all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{E}
            are skeletal. By
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            receives a surjective
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{E}
            from a Boolean topos.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            being skeletal and surjective implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(f)=\mathcal{E}
            is Boolean.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            A pullback characterisation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+geometric+morphisms"} open geometric morphisms
            from Johnstone (
            %a{:href => "#J06"}> 2006, cor. 4.9
            ):
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{E}
              is open iff its pullback along any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+geometric+morphism"} bounded geometric morphism
              with codomain
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is skeletal.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %h2#remark Remark
          %p
            Skeletal morphisms between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frames
            are studied in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Banaschewski_Pultr94"} Banaschewski-Pultr (1994
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Banaschewski_Pultr96"} 1996
            %em weakly open
            there.
          %p
            The equivalent concept for topological spaces appears in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Mioduszewski_Rudolf69"} Mioduszewski-Rudolf (1969)
          %p
            It is possible to define an analogous concept of
            %em m-skeletal geometric morphism
            using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morganization"} De Morgan topology
            on a topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+geometric+morphism"} open geometric morphism
            %li
